docs/architecture.md:75:Morph placements retain their unsigned 16-bit ratio. `morphShapeAtRatio` resolves geometry, bounds, colors, gradient matrices/stops/focal values, and line widths; previews and collision use the same resolved geometry as live views. `morph-raster.ts` rasterizes generated leaf paths synchronously with Canvas2D so frame changes do not wait for image decoding. Its recent-raster cache is limited to eight million pixels. Live morph textures are reference counted by `GameRenderer` and released when their last view changes ratio or is destroyed; static vector leaves remain shared for the run. Morph structure and edge pairing were cross-checked against [FFDec's morph implementation](https://github.com/jindrapetrik/jpexs-decompiler/blob/master/libsrc/ffdec_lib/src/com/jpexs/decompiler/flash/tags/base/MorphShapeTag.java).
docs/architecture.md:83:`simulation/editor-placement.ts` creates transient preview geometry, applies the three-pixel axis snap, and filters each dropped member against the design bounds. Palette choice, the in-progress preview gesture, and selection of placed parts are separate editor states. The browser component owns pointer capture and the keyboard alternative; dropping an object commits one design change without selecting it. Part radii come from authored symbol bounds, excluding the raster padding used for antialiasing.
docs/architecture.md:89:`render/creator-artwork.ts` reuses `SwfSymbolView` in one offscreen Pixi renderer, copying its transparent frame into the creator's design and preview canvases. Each placed part owns its own timeline birth frame; moving/scaling/selecting it does not recreate the clip. Dropping transfers draft views into the design so their animation continues. The frame clock follows the selected FPS, stops advancing while hidden, and stays outside React state. Pointer alpha tests consume each part's retained visible pose. Class measurement and saved designs continue to use their separate static snapshot and numerical geometry.
